---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-703 — TypeScript SDK quickstart. Companion to /docs/api-quickstart
// (raw HTTP / curl) and /docs/cli-quickstart. Pitched at teams that
// already have a Node/Bun/Deno service and want a typed surface
// instead of hand-rolling fetch calls. Posture matches V-680: code
// samples runnable verbatim once a key is in hand.
---

<BaseLayout title="TypeScript SDK quickstart — Driftstack" description="Use the Driftstack TypeScript SDK to start browser sessions, stream events, and pull recordings — with full type-safety.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>TypeScript SDK quickstart</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      The <code>@driftstack/sdk</code> package wraps the same REST
      surface as <a href="/docs/api-quickstart">the curl quickstart</a>,
      with end-to-end types generated from the OpenAPI spec. If you're
      already in a TS codebase, this is the recommended way to call
      Driftstack.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Install</h2>
    <pre><code>{`# bun
bun add @driftstack/sdk

# npm
npm install @driftstack/sdk

# pnpm
pnpm add @driftstack/sdk`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      Node ≥ 20, Bun ≥ 1.1, and Deno ≥ 1.40 are supported. The SDK
      ships ESM-only; if you're still on CommonJS, use a dynamic
      <code>import()</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Construct the client</h2>
    <pre><code>{`import { Driftstack } from '@driftstack/sdk';

const client = new Driftstack({
  apiKey: process.env.DRIFTSTACK_KEY!,
  // baseUrl defaults to https://api.driftstack.dev; override for
  // self-hosted deployments or staging.
});`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      The constructor does not make any network calls. Reuse one
      client across your process — it is internally pooled and safe
      for concurrent use.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Start a session</h2>
    <pre><code>{`const { session } = await client.sessions.start({
  target_url: 'https://example.com',
  profile_archetype: 'default',
});

console.log(session.id, session.status, session.live_url);`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      The return type is fully discriminated — TypeScript narrows
      <code>session.status</code> through the lifecycle (
      <code>queued</code> → <code>running</code> → <code>completed</code>
      / <code>failed</code>).
    </p>

    <h2>4. Wait for the session to finish</h2>
    <pre><code>{`const done = await client.sessions.waitUntilTerminal(session.id, {
  timeoutMs: 120_000,
});

if (done.status === 'completed') {
  const { playback_url } = await client.recordings.get(done.id);
  console.log('Recording:', playback_url);
}`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      <code>waitUntilTerminal</code> polls every 2 seconds with
      exponential backoff up to 10s. For production workloads, prefer
      webhooks (see <a href="/docs/webhooks">webhooks</a>) so you're
      not paying for idle polling.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Stream live events</h2>
    <pre><code>{`for await (const event of client.sessions.stream(session.id)) {
  if (event.type === 'navigation') {
    console.log('navigated to', event.url);
  }
}`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      The async-iterator is backed by Server-Sent Events. It
      reconnects automatically with exponential backoff on transient
      failures and closes when the session terminates.
    </p>

    <h2>Error handling</h2>
    <p>
      Every SDK method throws a typed <code>DriftstackError</code> on
      non-2xx responses. The error carries <code>status</code>,
      <code>code</code>, and <code>detail</code> so you can branch on
      structured causes:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`import { DriftstackError } from '@driftstack/sdk';

try {
  await client.sessions.start({ target_url: 'not-a-url' });
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof DriftstackError && err.code === 'invalid_request') {
    // user-facing validation error
  } else {
    throw err;
  }
}`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Bundled vs tree-shaken</h2>
    <p>
      The SDK is fully tree-shakeable. If you only import
      <code>client.sessions.*</code>, the recordings and billing
      modules never reach your bundle. Typical Next.js / Vite
      production bundles add ~12 kB gzipped.
    </p>

